{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And Laban came into Jacob's tent, and into Leah's tent.</b> After he mentioned 'and into Leah's tent' and 'into the tent of the two handmaids,' why was it necessary to say 'and he went out of Leah's tent'? He should have said 'and he went out of the tent of the two handmaids.'" "<b>But</b> it is possible to say that Leah's tent was the largest of them all, so that the tent of the two handmaids was within it; and from the time he went out of the tent of the two handmaids he was still standing in Leah's tent, and therefore it was necessary to say 'and he went out of Leah's tent.'" "<b>Now,</b> according to the opinion of Rashi, of blessed memory, there were all together three tents: for Jacob's tent is Rachel's tent, and Leah's tent — that makes two — and the tent of the two handmaids — that makes three tents. But according to the opinion of the Ramban, of blessed memory, there were five tents: Jacob's tent by itself, for there the women and children would eat with him at his table; and Leah's tent — that is two; and Rachel's tent — that is three; and the tent of the two handmaids, that is, for each of the two handmaids. And he said that this was on account of Jacob's modesty, that he should have four tents for the four women, a special tent for each, so that one would not know when he came to her fellow wife — for this is forbidden by Torah law."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 31, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:44.526020559Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}